            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Breyer noted that the Constitution\u2019s text delegates a plenary war\u2011power to the Federal Government, supporting the view that Congress may regulate matters affecting the military."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "0d9bd5ffa502bd21f5b05f50079d412e2fc00deb624222da935825c2ec2ba7fb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-0d9bd5ffa502-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The majority\u2019s holding expands the ability of service members to seek redress from state employers, reinforcing the federal government\u2019s role in supporting the armed forces."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Congress",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-4f97cb93343c-0004-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 86,
              "start": 86
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Congress, congress explicitly gave individuals a cause of action against states under USERRA, reflecting legislative intent to allow suits for military\u2011related discrimination."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-4f97cb93343c-0004-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 86,
              "start": 86
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Court noted that sovereign immunity generally shields states from private suits, highlighting the significance of the holding that creates an exception in this context."
      }
